{"id":"h031","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"A pure lead bar 10 cm long is maintained with one end at T &=300 K and the other at 310 K. The thermoelectric potential difference thus induced across the ends is 12.8 micro-volts. Find the thermoelectric power for lead in this temperature range in V/K. (Note: Q varies nonlinearly with temperature, but over this narrow temperature range, you may use a linear approximation.)\nIs the correct answer \"3.20e-07\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"Thermoelectric power is defined as S = ΔV/ΔT.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The given values are ΔV = 1.28e-5 V and ΔT = 10 K.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"S = 1.28e-5 V / 10 K = 1.28e-6 V/K.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The bar length of 10 cm does not enter the calculation.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"3.20e-7 V/K would require dividing by 40 K, which has no basis in the problem.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"3.20e-7 V/K is off from the correct result by a factor of 4.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"1.28 µV/K is consistent with lead's known thermoelectric power of about 1 µV/K near room temperature.","confidence":0.7,"lean":-0.6,"theta":1.0996,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The proposed answer 3.20e-7 is not correct.","confidence":0.96,"lean":-1,"theta":1.508,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":-0.3,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":2,"j":3,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":4,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":5,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"